(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("cicd",{"cicd/pipeline-fundamentals":{"html":"\u003cp>A team merges code on Friday, and an engineer spends the evening building, copying files to servers and hoping. The next release is delayed because nobody remembers the steps. A pipeline replaces that evening with an automated, repeatable path from a code change to a running service, and it does it the same way every time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between CI, continuous delivery and continuous deployment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The stages of a typical pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Principles of fast, reliable pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to measure whether delivery is healthy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-terms-often-confused\">Three terms, often confused\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Term\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Continuous integration (CI)\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every change is built and tested automatically, and merged into the shared mainline often\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Continuous delivery\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every passing change is ready to release, and a human decides when\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Continuous deployment\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every passing change is released to production automatically\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Most teams practise CI everywhere, continuous delivery as a baseline, and continuous deployment where their tests and monitoring are strong enough to trust it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stages-of-a-pipeline\">Stages of a pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Checkout\u003c/strong> the source at the exact revision.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Build\u003c/strong> the artifact once, for example a container image.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Test\u003c/strong>: fast unit tests first, then integration tests.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Analyze\u003c/strong>: linting, dependency checks, secret scanning, image scanning.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Publish\u003c/strong> the artifact to a registry with an immutable version tag.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Deploy\u003c/strong> to a test environment, run smoke tests.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Promote\u003c/strong> the \u003cem>same artifact\u003c/em> to production, then verify it.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Two rules deserve emphasis:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Build once, deploy many.\u003c/strong> The image tested in staging must be the image that reaches production. Rebuilding for each environment means you tested something different from what you shipped.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Order checks by speed.\u003c/strong> Put the quick, cheap feedback first (lint, unit tests) so a typo fails in seconds, not after a 20-minute build.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"principles\">Principles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Principle\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fast feedback\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Developers wait for pipelines. Aim for the main checks in under ten minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Deterministic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The same input gives the same result. Pin tool and dependency versions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fail loudly\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A red build must block the merge or deploy. Never leave a &quot;known flaky&quot; step ignored\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Secrets are managed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Credentials come from a secret store at run time, never from files in the source\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pipeline as code\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The definition lives next to the application, reviewed like any change\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Least privilege\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Build jobs can push images but should not be able to modify production directly\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measuring-delivery-health\">Measuring delivery health\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The four widely used delivery metrics (from DORA research) give you a shared language:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Metric\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Question it answers\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Deployment frequency\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How often do we release?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Lead time for changes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How long from commit to production?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Change failure rate\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>What share of releases cause a problem?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Time to restore service\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How fast do we recover when one does?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Frequent small releases generally improve \u003cstrong>all four\u003c/strong>, because small changes are easier to review, test and roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-tests-pass-production-breaks\">Incident: tests pass, production breaks\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The pipeline is green for version 2.4.0, but production returns errors immediately after the release. Staging was fine yesterday.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Compare what each environment actually runs:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-staging -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'\nkubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-prod -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-staging -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/shop-api:2.4.0-rc1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-prod -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/shop-api:2.4.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Staging ran a build from a release candidate, while production got a \u003cstrong>separate rebuild\u003c/strong> that picked up a newer dependency version. The artifact that was tested is not the artifact that shipped. Fix the pipeline so one build produces one image tagged with a unique revision, and every environment deploys that exact tag (or digest). Pin dependency versions with a lock file so rebuilds are reproducible. Roll production back to the last good tag while you do it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-pipeline\">Predict the pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pipeline has stages build, test and deploy. The test stage fails. What should happen to deploy?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It does not run, and the pipeline reports failure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Stages run in order and stop on failure. A broken change should never reach deployment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Two developers merge within seconds and both pipelines deploy to production. What risk appears?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The older build can finish last and overwrite the newer one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a concurrency group or a queue so only one deploy runs at a time, and deploy a specific version.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the artifact per environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a flaky test as noise instead of fixing or removing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the pipeline broad production credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping post-deploy verification\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-terms-often-confused","text":"Three terms, often confused"},{"id":"stages-of-a-pipeline","text":"Stages of a pipeline"},{"id":"principles","text":"Principles"},{"id":"measuring-delivery-health","text":"Measuring delivery health"},{"id":"incident-tests-pass-production-breaks","text":"Incident: tests pass, production breaks"},{"id":"predict-the-pipeline","text":"Predict the pipeline"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Martin Fowler: Continuous Integration","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/continuousIntegration.html"},{"t":"Continuous Delivery (Jez Humble and Dave Farley)","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"},{"t":"DORA research and metrics","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"A team merges code on Friday, and an engineer spends the evening building, copying files to servers and hoping. The next release is delayed because nobody remembers the steps. A pipeline replaces that evening with an automated, repeatable path from a code change to a running service, and it does it the same way every time. What you will learn The difference between CI, continuous delivery and continuous deployment The stages of a typical pipeline Principles of fast, reliable pipelines How to measure whether delivery is healthy Three terms, often confused Term Meaning Continuous integration (CI) Every change is built and tested automatically, and merged into the shared mainline often Continuous delivery Every passing change is ready to release, and a human decides when Continuous deployment Every passing change is released to production automatically Most teams practise CI everywhere, continuous delivery as a baseline, and continuous deployment where their tests and monitoring are strong enough to trust it. Stages of a pipeline Checkout the source at the exact revision. Build the artifact once, for example a container image. Test : fast unit tests first, then integration tests. Analyze : linting, dependency checks, secret scanning, image scanning. Publish the artifact to a registry with an immutable version tag. Deploy to a test environment, run smoke tests. Promote the same artifact to production, then verify it. Two rules deserve emphasis: Build once, deploy many. The image tested in staging must be the image that reaches production. Rebuilding for each environment means you tested something different from what you shipped. Order checks by speed. Put the quick, cheap feedback first (lint, unit tests) so a typo fails in seconds, not after a 20-minute build. Principles Principle Why Fast feedback Developers wait for pipelines. Aim for the main checks in under ten minutes Deterministic The same input gives the same result. Pin tool and dependency versions Fail loudly A red build must block the merge or deploy. Never leave a \"known flaky\" step ignored Secrets are managed Credentials come from a secret store at run time, never from files in the source Pipeline as code The definition lives next to the application, reviewed like any change Least privilege Build jobs can push images but should not be able to modify production directly Measuring delivery health The four widely used delivery metrics (from DORA research) give you a shared language: Metric Question it answers Deployment frequency How often do we release? Lead time for changes How long from commit to production? Change failure rate What share of releases cause a problem? Time to restore service How fast do we recover when one does? Frequent small releases generally improve all four , because small changes are easier to review, test and roll back. Incident: tests pass, production breaks Incident The pipeline is green for version 2.4.0, but production returns errors immediately after the release. Staging was fine yesterday. Compare what each environment actually runs: $ kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-staging -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27; registry.example.com/shop-api:2.4.0-rc1 $ kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-prod -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27; registry.example.com/shop-api:2.4.0 Staging ran a build from a release candidate, while production got a separate rebuild that picked up a newer dependency version. The artifact that was tested is not the artifact that shipped. Fix the pipeline so one build produces one image tagged with a unique revision, and every environment deploys that exact tag (or digest). Pin dependency versions with a lock file so rebuilds are reproducible. Roll production back to the last good tag while you do it. Predict the pipeline Predict first. A pipeline has stages build, test and deploy. The test stage fails. What should happen to deploy? Your guess Answer: It does not"},"cicd/building-a-pipeline":{"html":"\u003cp>Theory becomes useful when you can write the pipeline. This lesson builds a complete declarative Jenkins pipeline for a containerized service. The ideas carry over directly to other CI systems: stages, steps, environment, credentials, conditions and post-actions exist everywhere under different names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The structure of a declarative pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Credentials, parameters and conditions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parallel stages and post actions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to debug a failing build\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-complete-pipeline\">A complete pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"groovy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pipeline {\n  agent { label 'docker' }\n\n  options {\n    timeout(time: 30, unit: 'MINUTES')\n    disableConcurrentBuilds()\n    buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: '30'))\n  }\n\n  environment {\n    REGISTRY = 'registry.example.com'\n    IMAGE    = &quot;${REGISTRY}/shop-api&quot;\n    TAG      = &quot;build-${env.BUILD_NUMBER}&quot;\n  }\n\n  stages {\n    stage('Checkout') {\n      steps {\n        checkout scm\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Test') {\n      parallel {\n        stage('Unit tests') {\n          steps { sh 'make test' }\n        }\n        stage('Lint') {\n          steps { sh 'make lint' }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Build image') {\n      steps {\n        sh 'docker build -t &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot; .'\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Scan image') {\n      steps {\n        sh 'trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot;'\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Push') {\n      when { branch 'main' }\n      steps {\n        withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: 'registry-creds',\n                                          usernameVariable: 'REG_USER',\n                                          passwordVariable: 'REG_PASS')]) {\n          sh 'echo &quot;$REG_PASS&quot; | docker login &quot;$REGISTRY&quot; -u &quot;$REG_USER&quot; --password-stdin'\n          sh 'docker push &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot;'\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Deploy to staging') {\n      when { branch 'main' }\n      steps {\n        sh 'kubectl --context staging set image deployment/shop-api api=&quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot; -n shop'\n        sh 'kubectl --context staging rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop --timeout=180s'\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  post {\n    failure {\n      echo &quot;Build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} failed&quot;\n    }\n    always {\n      sh 'docker logout &quot;$REGISTRY&quot; || true'\n    }\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pipeline {\n  agent { label \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n\n  options {\n    timeout(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">time:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;MINUTES&#x27;\u003c/span>)\n    disableConcurrentBuilds()\n    buildDiscarder(logRotator(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">numToKeepStr:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;30&#x27;\u003c/span>))\n  }\n\n  environment {\n    REGISTRY = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;registry.example.com&#x27;\u003c/span>\n    IMAGE    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;${REGISTRY}/shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>\n    TAG      = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;build-${env.BUILD_NUMBER}&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n\n  stages {\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Checkout&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      steps {\n        checkout scm\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Test&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      parallel {\n        stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Unit tests&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n          steps { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;make test&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n        }\n        stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Lint&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n          steps { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;make lint&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Build image&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      steps {\n        sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker build -t &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot; .&#x27;\u003c/span>\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Scan image&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      steps {\n        sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span>\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Push&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      when { branch \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;main&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n      steps {\n        withCredentials([usernamePassword(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">credentialsId:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;registry-creds&#x27;\u003c/span>,\n                                          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-symbol\">usernameVariable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;REG_USER&#x27;\u003c/span>,\n                                          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-symbol\">passwordVariable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;REG_PASS&#x27;\u003c/span>)]) {\n          sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;echo &quot;$REG_PASS&quot; | docker login &quot;$REGISTRY&quot; -u &quot;$REG_USER&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\u003c/span>\n          sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker push &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span>\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Deploy to staging&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      when { branch \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;main&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n      steps {\n        sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;kubectl --context staging set image deployment/shop-api api=&quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot; -n shop&#x27;\u003c/span>\n        sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;kubectl --context staging rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop --timeout=180s&#x27;\u003c/span>\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  post {\n    failure {\n      echo \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} failed&quot;\u003c/span>\n    }\n    always {\n      sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker logout &quot;$REGISTRY&quot; || true&#x27;\u003c/span>\n    }\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-it\">Reading it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Part\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>agent\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Which worker runs the job\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>options\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Timeout, one build at a time, log retention\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>environment\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Variables for all stages. The image tag is unique per build, so every image is traceable to one run\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stages\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>steps\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The ordered work. \u003ccode>parallel\u003c/code> runs independent stages at the same time\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>when\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Condition. Push and deploy happen only on the main branch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>withCredentials\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Injects secrets for the block only and masks them in logs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>post\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runs after the stages: \u003ccode>always\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>failure\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>success\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Notice the single quotes around shell commands that use \u003ccode>$IMAGE\u003c/code>. With single quotes, the \u003cem>shell\u003c/em> expands the variable, so Jenkins never interpolates a secret into the command string. With double quotes, Groovy interpolates first and credentials can leak into the build log or process list.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not put credentials in the pipeline file, in environment variables defined in the file, or in the image. Store them in the CI system&#39;s credential store and reference them by ID. Give the registry credential push rights only to the repository it needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"habits-that-keep-pipelines-healthy\">Habits that keep pipelines healthy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keep the pipeline file small by moving logic into scripts (\u003ccode>make test\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>./ci/deploy.sh\u003c/code>) that developers can run locally the same way.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fail fast with \u003ccode>set -euo pipefail\u003c/code> in scripts, as in the scripting module.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache dependencies carefully and key the cache on the lock file.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Archive test reports and keep logs, so failures can be diagnosed afterward.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Verify after deploy. \u003ccode>rollout status\u003c/code> makes the pipeline fail if the new version never becomes ready.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-build-passes-locally-but-fails-in-ci\">Incident: the build passes locally but fails in CI\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>make test\u003c/code> passes on your laptop. In the pipeline the Test stage fails with \u003ccode>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named &#39;psycopg2&#39;\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The console log shows the exact command and the environment it ran in:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Pipeline] sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+ make test\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">python -m pytest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'psycopg2'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">make: *** [test] Error 1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Your laptop has the package installed globally from months ago; the clean CI worker does not. The pipeline had no step that installs the declared dependencies, so it relied on an accident. The cure is to make the build self-contained: install from the lock file in an isolated environment, and ideally run tests inside the same container image you build.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m venv .venv\n. .venv/bin/activate\npip install --require-hashes -r requirements.lock\npython -m pytest\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">python -m venv .venv\n. .venv/bin/activate\npip install --require-hashes -r requirements.lock\npython -m pytest\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A good rule: if a step only works on one machine, the pipeline has found an undeclared dependency. That is a feature of CI, not an annoyance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using double-quoted shell strings with secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeout, so a hung step blocks the agent forever\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deploying from any branch instead of only the main branch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tests that depend on tools installed manually on the worker\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-complete-pipeline","text":"A complete pipeline"},{"id":"reading-it","text":"Reading it"},{"id":"habits-that-keep-pipelines-healthy","text":"Habits that keep pipelines healthy"},{"id":"incident-the-build-passes-locally-but-fails-in-ci","text":"Incident: the build passes locally but fails in CI"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Jenkins: Pipeline syntax","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/syntax/"},{"t":"Jenkins documentation","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/"},{"t":"Jenkins: using credentials","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/using/using-credentials/"}],"text":"Theory becomes useful when you can write the pipeline. This lesson builds a complete declarative Jenkins pipeline for a containerized service. The ideas carry over directly to other CI systems: stages, steps, environment, credentials, conditions and post-actions exist everywhere under different names. What you will learn The structure of a declarative pipeline Credentials, parameters and conditions Parallel stages and post actions How to debug a failing build A complete pipeline pipeline { agent { label &#x27;docker&#x27; } options { timeout( time: 30 , unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27; ) disableConcurrentBuilds() buildDiscarder(logRotator( numToKeepStr: &#x27;30&#x27; )) } environment { REGISTRY = &#x27;registry.example.com&#x27; IMAGE = \"${REGISTRY}/shop-api\" TAG = \"build-${env.BUILD_NUMBER}\" } stages { stage( &#x27;Checkout&#x27; ) { steps { checkout scm } } stage( &#x27;Test&#x27; ) { parallel { stage( &#x27;Unit tests&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;make test&#x27; } } stage( &#x27;Lint&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;make lint&#x27; } } } } stage( &#x27;Build image&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;docker build -t \"$IMAGE:$TAG\" .&#x27; } } stage( &#x27;Scan image&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 \"$IMAGE:$TAG\"&#x27; } } stage( &#x27;Push&#x27; ) { when { branch &#x27;main&#x27; } steps { withCredentials([usernamePassword( credentialsId: &#x27;registry-creds&#x27; , usernameVariable: &#x27;REG_USER&#x27; , passwordVariable: &#x27;REG_PASS&#x27; )]) { sh &#x27;echo \"$REG_PASS\" | docker login \"$REGISTRY\" -u \"$REG_USER\" --password-stdin&#x27; sh &#x27;docker push \"$IMAGE:$TAG\"&#x27; } } } stage( &#x27;Deploy to staging&#x27; ) { when { branch &#x27;main&#x27; } steps { sh &#x27;kubectl --context staging set image deployment/shop-api api=\"$IMAGE:$TAG\" -n shop&#x27; sh &#x27;kubectl --context staging rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop --timeout=180s&#x27; } } } post { failure { echo \"Build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} failed\" } always { sh &#x27;docker logout \"$REGISTRY\" || true&#x27; } } } Reading it Part What it does agent Which worker runs the job options Timeout, one build at a time, log retention environment Variables for all stages. The image tag is unique per build, so every image is traceable to one run stages and steps The ordered work. parallel runs independent stages at the same time when Condition. Push and deploy happen only on the main branch withCredentials Injects secrets for the block only and masks them in logs post Runs after the stages: always , failure , success Notice the single quotes around shell commands that use $IMAGE . With single quotes, the shell expands the variable, so Jenkins never interpolates a secret into the command string. With double quotes, Groovy interpolates first and credentials can leak into the build log or process list. Warning Do not put credentials in the pipeline file, in environment variables defined in the file, or in the image. Store them in the CI system&#39;s credential store and reference them by ID. Give the registry credential push rights only to the repository it needs. Habits that keep pipelines healthy Keep the pipeline file small by moving logic into scripts ( make test , ./ci/deploy.sh ) that developers can run locally the same way. Fail fast with set -euo pipefail in scripts, as in the scripting module. Cache dependencies carefully and key the cache on the lock file. Archive test reports and keep logs, so failures can be diagnosed afterward. Verify after deploy. rollout status makes the pipeline fail if the new version never becomes ready. Incident: the build passes locally but fails in CI Incident make test passes on your laptop. In the pipeline the Test stage fails with ModuleNotFoundError: No module named &#39;psycopg2&#39; . The console log shows the exact command and the environment it ran in: [Pipeline] sh + make test python -m pytest ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'psycopg2' make: *** [test] Error 1 Your laptop has the package install"},"cicd/testing-and-quality-gates":{"html":"\u003cp>A pipeline that builds but does not test only delivers bugs faster. A pipeline with slow, flaky tests teaches people to ignore red builds. Good test strategy gives fast feedback first and expensive confidence later, and treats failures as information you can trust.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The test pyramid and what belongs at each level\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ordering pipeline stages for fast feedback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quality gates: linting, coverage thresholds and static analysis\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dealing with flaky tests and slow suites\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-test-pyramid\">The test pyramid\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it checks\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Speed\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>How many\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Unit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One function or class, no network or disk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Milliseconds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Thousands\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Integration\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A component with a real database, queue or API\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hundreds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>End to end\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The deployed system through its public interface\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A few dozen\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Smoke\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>&quot;Is the deployed service alive and answering?&quot;\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A handful\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The shape matters. Many fast unit tests find most defects cheaply. A few end-to-end tests prove the pieces work together. An inverted pyramid, with mostly slow browser tests, produces pipelines that take an hour and fail for reasons unrelated to the change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ordering-stages-for-fast-feedback\">Ordering stages for fast feedback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run the cheapest, most informative checks first and stop at the first failure. Run independent checks in parallel.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"stages: [lint, test, build, scan, deploy-staging, smoke]\n\nlint:\n  stage: lint\n  script:\n    - ruff check .\n    - yamllint deploy/\n    - hadolint Dockerfile\n\nunit:\n  stage: test\n  script:\n    - python -m pytest tests/unit -q --cov=app --cov-fail-under=80\n\nintegration:\n  stage: test\n  services:\n    - postgres:16\n  script:\n    - python -m pytest tests/integration -q\n\nbuild-image:\n  stage: build\n  script:\n    - docker build -t registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} .\n\nscan-image:\n  stage: scan\n  script:\n    - trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\n\nsmoke:\n  stage: smoke\n  script:\n    - curl -fsS -m 10 https://staging.example.com/healthz\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stages:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">lint\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">scan\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy-staging\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">smoke\u003c/span>]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">lint:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">lint\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ruff\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">check\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">yamllint\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy/\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">hadolint\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Dockerfile\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unit:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pytest\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tests/unit\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-q\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--cov=app\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--cov-fail-under=80\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">integration:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">services:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">postgres:16\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pytest\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tests/integration\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-q\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build-image:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docker\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-t\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scan-image:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">scan\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">trivy\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">image\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--exit-code\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--severity\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CRITICAL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">smoke:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">smoke\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">curl\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-fsS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://staging.example.com/healthz\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Lint finishes in seconds and catches typos and style problems before anyone waits for tests. The scan only runs on an image that built successfully. The smoke test runs against the freshly deployed staging environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quality-gates\">Quality gates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>gate\u003c/strong> is an automatic pass or fail rule. Useful gates include a minimum coverage threshold, no new high-severity static-analysis findings, no known critical vulnerabilities, and formatting that matches the project style.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m pytest --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under=80\nruff check . &amp;&amp; ruff format --check .\nshellcheck scripts/*.sh\nterraform fmt -check -recursive\nkubeconform -strict -summary k8s/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pytest --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under=80\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ruff check . &amp;&amp; ruff format --check .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>shellcheck scripts/*.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">fmt\u003c/span> -check -recursive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubeconform -strict -summary k8s/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Set gates you will actually enforce. A coverage target nobody respects is noise. Prefer &quot;coverage on changed lines must not fall&quot; over one global number, and avoid chasing 100 percent: tests that exist only to touch lines add cost without finding bugs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"flaky-tests\">Flaky tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A flaky test passes and fails with no code change. Common causes are timing assumptions, shared state between tests, order dependence, real network calls and clocks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Replace \u003ccode>sleep\u003c/code> with waiting for a condition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Give each test its own data and clean it up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Freeze or inject the clock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mock external services in unit tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run the suite in random order to expose hidden dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>When a flaky test is found, \u003cstrong>quarantine it quickly\u003c/strong>: move it to a non-blocking job, open a ticket with an owner and a deadline, and fix or delete it. Automatically re-running failures hides the problem and trains people to distrust red builds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keeping-the-pipeline-fast\">Keeping the pipeline fast\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m pytest -q --durations=10\npython -m pytest -q -n auto\ndocker build --cache-from registry.example.com/shop/api:cache -t shop-api:test .\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pytest -q --durations=10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pytest -q -n auto\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker build --cache-from registry.example.com/shop/api:cache -t shop-api:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> .\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Aim for the commit-to-feedback loop under ten minutes. Cache dependencies, run tests in parallel, run only affected tests where tooling supports it, and move slow suites to a nightly job while keeping a representative subset on every change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-green-build-that-broke-production\">Incident: the green build that broke production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change passes every check and deploys. Within minutes checkout fails: the new code reads a database column that exists in the test database but not in production, because a migration was never applied to the production schema.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Unit tests mocked the database, and the integration tests ran against a schema created fresh from the model, not from the real migration history. Close the gap:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python manage.py migrate --plan\npython manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run\ncurl -fsS https://staging.example.com/healthz/db\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python manage.py migrate --plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -fsS https://staging.example.com/healthz/db\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run integration tests against a database built by the same migrations that production uses, apply migrations in the pipeline before the application rollout, and add a smoke check that exercises a database-backed endpoint. Add a regression test for this exact case.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Letting the pipeline get slower until people skip it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automatically retrying flaky tests instead of fixing them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mocking everything so tests never touch real behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Coverage thresholds that are never enforced or are gamed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running end-to-end tests against shared, unstable environments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-test-pyramid","text":"The test pyramid"},{"id":"ordering-stages-for-fast-feedback","text":"Ordering stages for fast feedback"},{"id":"quality-gates","text":"Quality gates"},{"id":"flaky-tests","text":"Flaky tests"},{"id":"keeping-the-pipeline-fast","text":"Keeping the pipeline fast"},{"id":"incident-the-green-build-that-broke-production","text":"Incident: the green build that broke production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Martin Fowler: The practical test pyramid","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html"},{"t":"pytest documentation","u":"https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/"},{"t":"Grafana k6 documentation","u":"https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/"}],"text":"A pipeline that builds but does not test only delivers bugs faster. A pipeline with slow, flaky tests teaches people to ignore red builds. Good test strategy gives fast feedback first and expensive confidence later, and treats failures as information you can trust. What you will learn The test pyramid and what belongs at each level Ordering pipeline stages for fast feedback Quality gates: linting, coverage thresholds and static analysis Dealing with flaky tests and slow suites The test pyramid Level What it checks Speed How many Unit One function or class, no network or disk Milliseconds Thousands Integration A component with a real database, queue or API Seconds Hundreds End to end The deployed system through its public interface Minutes A few dozen Smoke \"Is the deployed service alive and answering?\" Seconds A handful The shape matters. Many fast unit tests find most defects cheaply. A few end-to-end tests prove the pieces work together. An inverted pyramid, with mostly slow browser tests, produces pipelines that take an hour and fail for reasons unrelated to the change. Ordering stages for fast feedback Run the cheapest, most informative checks first and stop at the first failure. Run independent checks in parallel. stages: [ lint , test , build , scan , deploy-staging , smoke ] lint: stage: lint script: - ruff check . - yamllint deploy/ - hadolint Dockerfile unit: stage: test script: - python -m pytest tests/unit -q --cov=app --cov-fail-under=80 integration: stage: test services: - postgres:16 script: - python -m pytest tests/integration -q build-image: stage: build script: - docker build -t registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} . scan-image: stage: scan script: - trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} smoke: stage: smoke script: - curl -fsS -m 10 https://staging.example.com/healthz Lint finishes in seconds and catches typos and style problems before anyone waits for tests. The scan only runs on an image that built successfully. The smoke test runs against the freshly deployed staging environment. Quality gates A gate is an automatic pass or fail rule. Useful gates include a minimum coverage threshold, no new high-severity static-analysis findings, no known critical vulnerabilities, and formatting that matches the project style. $ python -m pytest --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under=80 $ ruff check . && ruff format --check . $ shellcheck scripts/*.sh $ terraform fmt -check -recursive $ kubeconform -strict -summary k8s/ Set gates you will actually enforce. A coverage target nobody respects is noise. Prefer \"coverage on changed lines must not fall\" over one global number, and avoid chasing 100 percent: tests that exist only to touch lines add cost without finding bugs. Flaky tests A flaky test passes and fails with no code change. Common causes are timing assumptions, shared state between tests, order dependence, real network calls and clocks. Replace sleep with waiting for a condition Give each test its own data and clean it up Freeze or inject the clock Mock external services in unit tests Run the suite in random order to expose hidden dependencies When a flaky test is found, quarantine it quickly : move it to a non-blocking job, open a ticket with an owner and a deadline, and fix or delete it. Automatically re-running failures hides the problem and trains people to distrust red builds. Keeping the pipeline fast $ python -m pytest -q --durations=10 $ python -m pytest -q -n auto $ docker build --cache-from registry.example.com/shop/api:cache -t shop-api: test . Aim for the commit-to-feedback loop under ten minutes. Cache dependencies, run tests in parallel, run only affected tests where tooling supports it, and move slow suites to a nightly job while keeping a representative subset on every change. Incident: the green build that broke production Incident A change passes every check and deploys. Within minutes checkout fails: the new code reads a database "},"cicd/deployment-strategies":{"html":"\u003cp>How you put a new version in front of users decides how much risk each release carries. A strategy answers three questions: how much traffic is exposed to the new version, how quickly can you go back, and how much extra capacity does it cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rolling, blue-green and canary strategies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Feature flags and why deploy is not release\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe handling of database changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automated verification and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-strategies\">The strategies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Strength\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Weakness\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Recreate\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stop the old version, start the new one\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simplest\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Downtime\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Rolling\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replace instances a few at a time\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No extra capacity, no downtime\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Old and new run together for a while; slower rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Blue-green\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Run the new version beside the old, then switch all traffic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Instant switch and instant rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Double capacity during the switch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Canary\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Send a small share of traffic (say 5%) to the new version, then grow it\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Limits the damage of a bad release\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Needs traffic control and good metrics\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>In Kubernetes, a Deployment gives you rolling updates by default. Blue-green is commonly done by running two Deployments and switching a Service selector. Canary needs an ingress or service mesh that can split traffic by weight, or a progressive delivery controller.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Blue-green switch: change which version the Service selects\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: shop-api\n    track: green        # was: blue\n  ports:\n    - port: 80\n      targetPort: 8080\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Blue-green switch: change which version the Service selects\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Service\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">track:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">green\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># was: blue\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetPort:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-canary-needs-a-decision-rule\">A canary needs a decision rule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A canary is only as good as the metrics you check. Decide in advance what &quot;healthy&quot; means and let automation judge it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Error rate of the canary is no worse than the stable version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Latency at the 95th percentile within an agreed margin\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No increase in crashes or restarts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>If any check fails, traffic returns to the stable version automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"separate-deploy-from-release\">Separate deploy from release\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>feature flag\u003c/strong> lets you put code into production switched off, then turn it on for a small group, then everyone. Deploying becomes routine and low-risk, and &quot;release&quot; becomes a business decision that can be reversed in seconds without redeploying.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"if flags.is_enabled(&quot;new-checkout&quot;, user_id=user.id):\n    return new_checkout(cart)\nreturn old_checkout(cart)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> flags.is_enabled(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new-checkout&quot;\u003c/span>, user_id=user.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> new_checkout(cart)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> old_checkout(cart)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Remove flags once a feature is fully released. Old flags become hidden complexity that nobody dares touch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"databases-are-the-hard-part\">Databases are the hard part\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Code can be rolled back in seconds; data changes cannot. During a rolling or canary release both versions run against the same database, so every schema change must work with \u003cstrong>both\u003c/strong> the old and new code. Use the \u003cstrong>expand and contract\u003c/strong> pattern:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Expand\u003c/strong>: add the new column or table (nullable, with no breaking change). Old code ignores it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Migrate\u003c/strong>: deploy code that writes both and reads the new one; backfill existing data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Contract\u003c/strong>: after the old version is gone and you are sure you will not roll back, remove the old column in a later release.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Never rename or drop a column in the same release that stops using it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verify-and-roll-back\">Verify and roll back\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After every deploy, run automated \u003cstrong>smoke tests\u003c/strong> against the real environment and watch key metrics for a short bake time. Define the rollback in advance: for Kubernetes, \u003ccode>kubectl rollout undo\u003c/code>; for blue-green, switch the selector back; for flags, turn the flag off. A rollback you have never practised is a guess.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-canary-takes-down-checkout\">Incident: a canary takes down checkout\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A canary at 10% of traffic goes out at 14:00. At 14:06 the checkout error rate rises to 12% for the whole site, not just the canary.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Look at what the canary and stable versions have in common: the database.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs deploy/shop-api-canary -n shop --tail 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/shop-api-canary -n shop --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR: column &quot;currency_code&quot; of relation &quot;orders&quot; does not exist\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new code expects a column that the migration has not created in this environment, and a second effect: the canary&#39;s failed requests hold database connections, starving the stable pods. Immediately set the canary weight to zero to stop the damage, which restores service.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The lessons are procedural: run the migration (the \u003cstrong>expand\u003c/strong> step) as its own earlier release and confirm it before the code that needs it, add a rule that aborts the canary automatically when the error rate climbs above a threshold, and keep connection pools sized so one bad version cannot exhaust the database.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"new&gt;five;five&gt;gate;gate&gt;full;gate&gt;back\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A canary release step by step\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"new\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">New version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"five\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">5 percent traffic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gate\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Automatic check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"full\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Full rollout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"back\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rollback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, New version to 5 percent traffic; 2, 5 percent traffic to Automatic check; 3, Automatic check to Full rollout; 4, Automatic check to Rollback.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"new\">\u003cdt>New version \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Deployed beside the old one with no traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it fails to start, nothing is lost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"five\">\u003cdt>5 percent traffic \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A small share of users reach it while metrics are watched.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without metrics you cannot see harm.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gate\">\u003cdt>Automatic check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare error rate and latency with the old version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A weak check lets a bad build through.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"full\">\u003cdt>Full rollout \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Traffic moves up in steps to 100 percent.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping steps removes the safety.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"back\">\u003cdt>Rollback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Traffic returns to the old version in seconds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A migration that is not reversible makes rollback unsafe.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No rollback plan, or one that was never tried\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Releasing database changes together with code that depends on them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canaries without metrics or automatic abort\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving feature flags in the code for years\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-strategies","text":"The strategies"},{"id":"a-canary-needs-a-decision-rule","text":"A canary needs a decision rule"},{"id":"separate-deploy-from-release","text":"Separate deploy from release"},{"id":"databases-are-the-hard-part","text":"Databases are the hard part"},{"id":"verify-and-roll-back","text":"Verify and roll back"},{"id":"incident-a-canary-takes-down-checkout","text":"Incident: a canary takes down checkout"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Deployments","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/"},{"t":"Argo Rollouts documentation","u":"https://argo-rollouts.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"Continuous Delivery (Jez Humble and Dave Farley)","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"}],"text":"How you put a new version in front of users decides how much risk each release carries. A strategy answers three questions: how much traffic is exposed to the new version, how quickly can you go back, and how much extra capacity does it cost. What you will learn Rolling, blue-green and canary strategies Feature flags and why deploy is not release Safe handling of database changes Automated verification and rollback The strategies Strategy How it works Strength Weakness Recreate Stop the old version, start the new one Simplest Downtime Rolling Replace instances a few at a time No extra capacity, no downtime Old and new run together for a while; slower rollback Blue-green Run the new version beside the old, then switch all traffic Instant switch and instant rollback Double capacity during the switch Canary Send a small share of traffic (say 5%) to the new version, then grow it Limits the damage of a bad release Needs traffic control and good metrics In Kubernetes, a Deployment gives you rolling updates by default. Blue-green is commonly done by running two Deployments and switching a Service selector. Canary needs an ingress or service mesh that can split traffic by weight, or a progressive delivery controller. # Blue-green switch: change which version the Service selects apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: shop-api spec: selector: app: shop-api track: green # was: blue ports: - port: 80 targetPort: 8080 A canary needs a decision rule A canary is only as good as the metrics you check. Decide in advance what \"healthy\" means and let automation judge it: Error rate of the canary is no worse than the stable version Latency at the 95th percentile within an agreed margin No increase in crashes or restarts If any check fails, traffic returns to the stable version automatically. Separate deploy from release A feature flag lets you put code into production switched off, then turn it on for a small group, then everyone. Deploying becomes routine and low-risk, and \"release\" becomes a business decision that can be reversed in seconds without redeploying. if flags.is_enabled( \"new-checkout\" , user_id=user. id ): return new_checkout(cart) return old_checkout(cart) Remove flags once a feature is fully released. Old flags become hidden complexity that nobody dares touch. Databases are the hard part Code can be rolled back in seconds; data changes cannot. During a rolling or canary release both versions run against the same database, so every schema change must work with both the old and new code. Use the expand and contract pattern: Expand : add the new column or table (nullable, with no breaking change). Old code ignores it. Migrate : deploy code that writes both and reads the new one; backfill existing data. Contract : after the old version is gone and you are sure you will not roll back, remove the old column in a later release. Never rename or drop a column in the same release that stops using it. Verify and roll back After every deploy, run automated smoke tests against the real environment and watch key metrics for a short bake time. Define the rollback in advance: for Kubernetes, kubectl rollout undo ; for blue-green, switch the selector back; for flags, turn the flag off. A rollback you have never practised is a guess. Incident: a canary takes down checkout Incident A canary at 10% of traffic goes out at 14:00. At 14:06 the checkout error rate rises to 12% for the whole site, not just the canary. Look at what the canary and stable versions have in common: the database. $ kubectl logs deploy/shop-api-canary -n shop -- tail 3 ERROR: column \"currency_code\" of relation \"orders\" does not exist The new code expects a column that the migration has not created in this environment, and a second effect: the canary&#39;s failed requests hold database connections, starving the stable pods. Immediately set the canary weight to zero to stop the damage, which restores service. The lessons are procedural: run the migration (the expand step) as"},"cicd/versioning-and-artifacts":{"html":"\u003cp>The package you test must be the package you deploy. If staging runs one build and production runs another built later from the same source, you have tested something else. Artifacts, versions and promotion rules make releases predictable and reversible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Build once, promote everywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Semantic versioning and build metadata\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing artifacts and caching dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changelogs, release notes and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-once-promote-the-artifact\">Build once, promote the artifact\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pipeline should produce an \u003cstrong>immutable artifact\u003c/strong> (a container image, a package, a binary) exactly once. Later stages deploy that same artifact, changing only configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"commit -&gt; build artifact (shop-api:1.8.2-b457) -&gt; test -&gt; staging -&gt; approve -&gt; production\n                              |                                         ^\n                              +--------- same artifact promoted --------+\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">commit -&gt; build artifact (shop-api:1.8.2-b457) -&gt; test -&gt; staging -&gt; approve -&gt; production\n                              |                                         ^\n                              +--------- same artifact promoted --------+\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Environment differences belong in configuration: values, environment variables, secrets and manifests. If you rebuild per environment, a different dependency version or timestamp can sneak in, and &quot;it passed in staging&quot; stops meaning anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"semantic-versioning\">Semantic versioning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>SemVer uses \u003ccode>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\u003c/code>: increase \u003cstrong>MAJOR\u003c/strong> for incompatible changes, \u003cstrong>MINOR\u003c/strong> for backward-compatible features and \u003cstrong>PATCH\u003c/strong> for backward-compatible fixes. Pre-release and build information go after: \u003ccode>2.1.0-rc.1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>2.1.0+b457\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"VERSION=$(cat VERSION)\nBUILD=${BUILD_NUMBER:-0}\nTAG=&quot;${VERSION}-b${BUILD}&quot;\necho &quot;building ${TAG}&quot;\ndocker build --build-arg VERSION=&quot;$VERSION&quot; -t registry.example.com/shop/api:&quot;$TAG&quot; .\ndocker push registry.example.com/shop/api:&quot;$TAG&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>VERSION=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> VERSION)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>BUILD=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${BUILD_NUMBER:-0}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>TAG=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${VERSION}\u003c/span>-b\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${BUILD}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;building \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${TAG}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker build --build-arg VERSION=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$VERSION\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -t registry.example.com/shop/api:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$TAG\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker push registry.example.com/shop/api:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$TAG\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Services that only talk to your own clients often use a build number or date instead. What matters is that every deployed artifact has a \u003cstrong>unique, traceable identifier\u003c/strong> that maps back to the exact source revision, so you can answer &quot;what is running in production&quot; in seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recording-what-was-built\">Recording what was built\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store metadata with the artifact: source revision, build number, build time, test results and scan results. Embed the version in the application so it can report it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"build:\n  script:\n    - docker build -t registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\n        --label org.opencontainers.image.revision=${SOURCE_REV}\n        --label org.opencontainers.image.version=${VERSION} .\n    - docker push registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\n  artifacts:\n    paths:\n      - sbom.json\n      - test-report.xml\n    expire_in: 90 days\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docker\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-t\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--label\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">org.opencontainers.image.revision=${SOURCE_REV}\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--label\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">org.opencontainers.image.version=${VERSION}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docker\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">push\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">artifacts:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sbom.json\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test-report.xml\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expire_in:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">days\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker inspect registry.example.com/shop/api:457 --format '{{json .Config.Labels}}'\ncurl -fsS https://shop.example.com/version\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect registry.example.com/shop/api:457 --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{json .Config.Labels}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -fsS https://shop.example.com/version\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>/version\u003c/code> endpoint, or a metric like \u003ccode>app_build_info{version=&quot;1.8.2&quot;}\u003c/code>, lets monitoring show which version served each request and correlate errors with releases.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caches-and-artifact-storage\">Caches and artifact storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dependency downloads dominate build time. Cache them keyed on the lock file so the cache refreshes only when dependencies change.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cache:\n  key:\n    files: [requirements.txt]\n  paths:\n    - .cache/pip\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cache:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">files:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">requirements.txt\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.cache/pip\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use a proper artifact repository for packages and images, with retention rules. Keep release artifacts long enough to roll back and to satisfy audit needs, and delete short-lived branch builds quickly. Never rely on a build agent&#39;s local disk as the only copy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"changelog-and-rollback\">Changelog and rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Automate release notes from the changes included, and require a rollback plan for risky ones. The simplest rollback is redeploying the previous artifact, which is only possible if:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>the previous artifact still exists in the registry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>database changes were backward compatible (expand, then contract)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>configuration for the old version is still available\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop --to-revision=12\nkubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop --to-revision=12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-nobody-knows-what-is-in-production\">Incident: nobody knows what is in production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A customer reports a bug that was fixed last week. Support says the fix is released. Engineers cannot tell whether production runs the fix because images are tagged \u003ccode>prod\u003c/code> and the pipeline rebuilt from the main branch during the last deployment.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Without a unique version on the running artifact there is no way to answer. The recovery path and prevention:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'\nkubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/shop/api:prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.items[0].status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Resolve the digest to a build, then to a source revision via the labels. From now on, tag with a unique build identifier, never rebuild for promotion, and surface the version in the UI footer and metrics.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the artifact for each environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mutable tags such as \u003ccode>prod\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> as release identifiers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cleaning up old artifacts so a rollback is impossible\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Breaking database compatibility so the previous version cannot run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping the only copy of an artifact on a build agent\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"build-once-promote-the-artifact","text":"Build once, promote the artifact"},{"id":"semantic-versioning","text":"Semantic versioning"},{"id":"recording-what-was-built","text":"Recording what was built"},{"id":"caches-and-artifact-storage","text":"Caches and artifact storage"},{"id":"changelog-and-rollback","text":"Changelog and rollback"},{"id":"incident-nobody-knows-what-is-in-production","text":"Incident: nobody knows what is in production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Semantic Versioning 2.0.0","u":"https://semver.org/"},{"t":"Keep a Changelog","u":"https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/"},{"t":"DORA research and metrics","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"The package you test must be the package you deploy. If staging runs one build and production runs another built later from the same source, you have tested something else. Artifacts, versions and promotion rules make releases predictable and reversible. What you will learn Build once, promote everywhere Semantic versioning and build metadata Storing artifacts and caching dependencies Changelogs, release notes and rollback Build once, promote the artifact A pipeline should produce an immutable artifact (a container image, a package, a binary) exactly once. Later stages deploy that same artifact, changing only configuration. commit -> build artifact (shop-api:1.8.2-b457) -> test -> staging -> approve -> production | ^ +--------- same artifact promoted --------+ Environment differences belong in configuration: values, environment variables, secrets and manifests. If you rebuild per environment, a different dependency version or timestamp can sneak in, and \"it passed in staging\" stops meaning anything. Semantic versioning SemVer uses MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH : increase MAJOR for incompatible changes, MINOR for backward-compatible features and PATCH for backward-compatible fixes. Pre-release and build information go after: 2.1.0-rc.1 , 2.1.0+b457 . $ VERSION=$( cat VERSION) $ BUILD= ${BUILD_NUMBER:-0} $ TAG= \" ${VERSION} -b ${BUILD} \" $ echo \"building ${TAG} \" $ docker build --build-arg VERSION= \" $VERSION \" -t registry.example.com/shop/api: \" $TAG \" . $ docker push registry.example.com/shop/api: \" $TAG \" Services that only talk to your own clients often use a build number or date instead. What matters is that every deployed artifact has a unique, traceable identifier that maps back to the exact source revision, so you can answer \"what is running in production\" in seconds. Recording what was built Store metadata with the artifact: source revision, build number, build time, test results and scan results. Embed the version in the application so it can report it. build: script: - docker build -t registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} --label org.opencontainers.image.revision=${SOURCE_REV} --label org.opencontainers.image.version=${VERSION} . - docker push registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} artifacts: paths: - sbom.json - test-report.xml expire_in: 90 days $ docker inspect registry.example.com/shop/api:457 --format &#x27;{{json .Config.Labels}}&#x27; $ curl -fsS https://shop.example.com/version A /version endpoint, or a metric like app_build_info{version=\"1.8.2\"} , lets monitoring show which version served each request and correlate errors with releases. Caches and artifact storage Dependency downloads dominate build time. Cache them keyed on the lock file so the cache refreshes only when dependencies change. cache: key: files: [ requirements.txt ] paths: - .cache/pip Use a proper artifact repository for packages and images, with retention rules. Keep release artifacts long enough to roll back and to satisfy audit needs, and delete short-lived branch builds quickly. Never rely on a build agent&#39;s local disk as the only copy. Changelog and rollback Automate release notes from the changes included, and require a rollback plan for risky ones. The simplest rollback is redeploying the previous artifact, which is only possible if: the previous artifact still exists in the registry database changes were backward compatible (expand, then contract) configuration for the old version is still available $ kubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop $ kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop --to-revision=12 $ kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop Incident: nobody knows what is in production Incident A customer reports a bug that was fixed last week. Support says the fix is released. Engineers cannot tell whether production runs the fix because images are tagged prod and the pipeline rebuilt from the main branch during the last deployment. Without a unique version on the running artifact there is no way to answer"},"cicd/pipeline-security":{"html":"\u003cp>A pipeline has the keys to production: it can read your source, build what you run and deploy it. That makes it one of the most attractive targets in your company. Attackers who compromise a pipeline do not need to break production, because the pipeline will deploy their code for them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Handling secrets without exposing them in logs or code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Isolating and hardening build runners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting deploy credentials with least privilege and short lifetimes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning code, dependencies and images, and pinning what you consume\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-in-pipelines\">Secrets in pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Secrets must come from a secret store or the CI system&#39;s protected variables, be masked in logs and be scoped to the jobs that need them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"deploy-prod:\n  stage: deploy\n  environment: production\n  rules:\n    - if: '$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == &quot;main&quot;'\n  variables:\n    AWS_ROLE_ARN: &quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-deploy&quot;\n  id_tokens:\n    CI_JOB_JWT:\n      aud: sts.amazonaws.com\n  script:\n    - aws sts assume-role-with-web-identity --role-arn &quot;$AWS_ROLE_ARN&quot; --role-session-name ci-$BUILD_ID --web-identity-token &quot;$CI_JOB_JWT&quot; --duration-seconds 900\n    - ./deploy.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy-prod:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">if:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == &quot;main&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">variables:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">AWS_ROLE_ARN:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-deploy&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">id_tokens:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">CI_JOB_JWT:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">aud:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sts.amazonaws.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">aws\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sts\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">assume-role-with-web-identity\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--role-arn\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$AWS_ROLE_ARN&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--role-session-name\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci-$BUILD_ID\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--web-identity-token\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$CI_JOB_JWT&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--duration-seconds\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">900\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./deploy.sh\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The best secret is the one that does not exist. \u003cstrong>Workload identity federation\u003c/strong> (OIDC) lets the pipeline prove who it is to the cloud provider and receive credentials that live for minutes, so there is no stored access key to leak. Where static secrets are unavoidable, restrict them to protected branches, rotate them regularly and never echo them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -rnE &quot;AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|BEGIN (RSA|EC|OPENSSH) PRIVATE KEY&quot; . --include=* -I | head\ndetect-secrets scan --all-files | head -20\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rnE \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|BEGIN (RSA|EC|OPENSSH) PRIVATE KEY&quot;\u003c/span> . --include=* -I | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>detect-secrets scan --all-files | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -20\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run a secret scanner in the pipeline and as a pre-commit hook. A leaked key must be treated as compromised: revoke it first, then clean the history.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runners-and-isolation\">Runners and isolation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Build agents run untrusted code, since any pull request may contain a malicious test or build script. Treat them accordingly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use ephemeral runners that are destroyed after each job\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Give runners no standing access to production networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate runners for untrusted contributions from those with deploy credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do not run pull request pipelines from forks with secrets available\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep the runner software and base image patched\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege-for-deployment\">Least privilege for deployment\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The deploy job needs permission to update specific workloads, not to administer the account.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  name: deployer\n  namespace: shop\nrules:\n  - apiGroups: [&quot;apps&quot;]\n    resources: [&quot;deployments&quot;]\n    verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;patch&quot;, &quot;update&quot;]\n  - apiGroups: [&quot;&quot;]\n    resources: [&quot;pods&quot;, &quot;pods/log&quot;]\n    verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Role\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployer\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroups:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;apps&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployments&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verbs:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;patch&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;update&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroups:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods/log&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verbs:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Require approval for production, restrict who can approve, and make the pipeline definition itself reviewable. If anyone can edit the pipeline file in a feature branch and have it run with production credentials, protection of the main branch is meaningless.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"securing-what-you-consume\">Securing what you consume\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dependencies are code you did not write. Attackers publish look-alike package names, take over abandoned packages and add malicious install scripts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"npm ci --ignore-scripts\npython -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt\npip-audit -r requirements.txt\nnpm audit --audit-level=high\ntrivy fs --scanners vuln,secret,misconfig .\ntrivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL Dockerfile k8s/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>npm ci --ignore-scripts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pip-audit -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>npm audit --audit-level=high\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy fs --scanners vuln,secret,misconfig .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL Dockerfile k8s/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use lock files and install exactly what they list. Pull through an internal proxy or mirror. Pin third-party CI plugins and actions to an exact version or digest, because a tag can be moved. Generate an SBOM for every release and sign artifacts so deployment can verify them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-layered-pipeline\">A layered pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Stage\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Control\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Commit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Secret scanning, signed commits, branch protection and review\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Build\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Ephemeral runner, pinned base images, locked dependencies\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Test\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Static analysis (SAST), dependency audit, IaC misconfiguration scan\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Package\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Image scan, SBOM, signature\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Deploy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Admission policy verifies signature, OIDC short-lived credentials, approvals\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Run\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runtime monitoring, audit logs, network policy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>No single control is enough. Each layer assumes another one may fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-leaked-deploy-key\">Incident: the leaked deploy key\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A contractor&#39;s pull request adds a debug line \u003ccode>env | sort\u003c/code> to a test script. The pipeline runs it and prints every environment variable into a publicly viewable log, including a long-lived cloud access key with admin rights. Within an hour, unfamiliar instances start in an unused region.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Respond in order: revoke, contain, investigate, then harden.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam update-access-key --access-key-id AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 --status Inactive --user-name ci-deployer\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 --max-results 20\naws ec2 describe-instances --region ap-southeast-2 --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam update-access-key --access-key-id AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 --status Inactive --user-name ci-deployer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 --max-results 20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-instances --region ap-southeast-2 --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deactivate the key, review everything it did, terminate rogue resources, and delete the log. Then fix the design: replace the static key with OIDC federation, scope the role to deploy only, stop exposing secrets to pull request pipelines, and add a secret scanner that blocks the merge.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long-lived admin keys stored as CI variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running untrusted pull requests with production secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Persistent runners that keep state and credentials between jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using floating tags for third-party pipeline plugins\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating the pipeline configuration as less important than application code\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"secrets-in-pipelines","text":"Secrets in pipelines"},{"id":"runners-and-isolation","text":"Runners and isolation"},{"id":"least-privilege-for-deployment","text":"Least privilege for deployment"},{"id":"securing-what-you-consume","text":"Securing what you consume"},{"id":"a-layered-pipeline","text":"A layered pipeline"},{"id":"incident-the-leaked-deploy-key","text":"Incident: the leaked deploy key"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OWASP Top 10 CI/CD Security Risks","u":"https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-ci-cd-security-risks/"},{"t":"SLSA: supply-chain levels for software artifacts","u":"https://slsa.dev/"},{"t":"AWS IAM: OIDC identity providers","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_providers_oidc.html"}],"text":"A pipeline has the keys to production: it can read your source, build what you run and deploy it. That makes it one of the most attractive targets in your company. Attackers who compromise a pipeline do not need to break production, because the pipeline will deploy their code for them. What you will learn Handling secrets without exposing them in logs or code Isolating and hardening build runners Granting deploy credentials with least privilege and short lifetimes Scanning code, dependencies and images, and pinning what you consume Secrets in pipelines Secrets must come from a secret store or the CI system&#39;s protected variables, be masked in logs and be scoped to the jobs that need them. deploy-prod: stage: deploy environment: production rules: - if: &#x27;$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == \"main\"&#x27; variables: AWS_ROLE_ARN: \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-deploy\" id_tokens: CI_JOB_JWT: aud: sts.amazonaws.com script: - aws sts assume-role-with-web-identity --role-arn \"$AWS_ROLE_ARN\" --role-session-name ci-$BUILD_ID --web-identity-token \"$CI_JOB_JWT\" --duration-seconds 900 - ./deploy.sh The best secret is the one that does not exist. Workload identity federation (OIDC) lets the pipeline prove who it is to the cloud provider and receive credentials that live for minutes, so there is no stored access key to leak. Where static secrets are unavoidable, restrict them to protected branches, rotate them regularly and never echo them. $ grep -rnE \"AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|BEGIN (RSA|EC|OPENSSH) PRIVATE KEY\" . --include=* -I | head $ detect-secrets scan --all-files | head -20 Run a secret scanner in the pipeline and as a pre-commit hook. A leaked key must be treated as compromised: revoke it first, then clean the history. Runners and isolation Build agents run untrusted code, since any pull request may contain a malicious test or build script. Treat them accordingly. Use ephemeral runners that are destroyed after each job Give runners no standing access to production networks Separate runners for untrusted contributions from those with deploy credentials Do not run pull request pipelines from forks with secrets available Keep the runner software and base image patched Least privilege for deployment The deploy job needs permission to update specific workloads, not to administer the account. apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata: name: deployer namespace: shop rules: - apiGroups: [ \"apps\" ] resources: [ \"deployments\" ] verbs: [ \"get\" , \"list\" , \"patch\" , \"update\" ] - apiGroups: [ \"\" ] resources: [ \"pods\" , \"pods/log\" ] verbs: [ \"get\" , \"list\" ] Require approval for production, restrict who can approve, and make the pipeline definition itself reviewable. If anyone can edit the pipeline file in a feature branch and have it run with production credentials, protection of the main branch is meaningless. Securing what you consume Dependencies are code you did not write. Attackers publish look-alike package names, take over abandoned packages and add malicious install scripts. $ npm ci --ignore-scripts $ python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt $ pip-audit -r requirements.txt $ npm audit --audit-level=high $ trivy fs --scanners vuln,secret,misconfig . $ trivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL Dockerfile k8s/ Use lock files and install exactly what they list. Pull through an internal proxy or mirror. Pin third-party CI plugins and actions to an exact version or digest, because a tag can be moved. Generate an SBOM for every release and sign artifacts so deployment can verify them. A layered pipeline Stage Control Commit Secret scanning, signed commits, branch protection and review Build Ephemeral runner, pinned base images, locked dependencies Test Static analysis (SAST), dependency audit, IaC misconfiguration scan Package Image scan, SBOM, signature Deploy Admission policy verifies signature, OIDC short-lived credentials, approvals Run Runtime monitoring, audit logs, network policy No single control is enough. Each la"},"cicd/declarative-delivery-with-argo-cd":{"html":"\u003cp>In a push pipeline, your CI system holds credentials to the cluster and runs \u003ccode>kubectl apply\u003c/code>. That works, but those credentials are a target, nobody notices when someone changes the cluster by hand, and the real state of production lives in the memory of whoever ran the last command. \u003cstrong>Declarative delivery\u003c/strong> reverses the direction: a controller inside the cluster pulls the desired configuration from a reviewed source and keeps the cluster matching it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The pull model and its benefits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How Argo CD and Flux work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>An Argo CD Application\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sync, drift correction and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"push-versus-pull\">Push versus pull\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Push (pipeline applies)\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Pull (controller applies)\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cluster credentials\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Held by the CI system\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stay inside the cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Manual changes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Remain until the next deploy, unnoticed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Detected and reverted or reported\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Source of truth\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The last pipeline run\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The reviewed configuration\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Audit trail\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CI logs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Change history of the configuration\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Re-run an older pipeline\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Revert the change; the controller reconciles\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is the same reconcile loop you met in the first Kubernetes lesson, applied to \u003cem>deployment itself\u003c/em>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-flow\">The flow\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>CI builds and tests the application and publishes an image with a unique tag.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A change to the \u003cstrong>configuration repository\u003c/strong> updates the image tag for an environment. This is a normal reviewed change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The controller notices the new desired state, compares it with the cluster and applies the difference.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>It reports \u003ccode>Synced\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Healthy\u003c/code>, or shows exactly what differs.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Keep \u003cstrong>application source\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>deployment configuration\u003c/strong> in separate repositories when you can. That stops every code commit from being a deploy request, and lets a platform team control environments without touching application code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"argo-cd-and-flux\">Argo CD and Flux\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Both are widely used, CNCF-hosted controllers for this model.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Argo CD\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Flux\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Style\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application-centric with a web UI and CLI\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Set of controllers driven by Kubernetes objects\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Strength\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Visual view of sync and health; easy to explore\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Composable and lightweight; strong automation of image updates\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Config unit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Application\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A source object, \u003ccode>Kustomization\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>HelmRelease\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Choose either; the concepts are identical. The example below uses Argo CD.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api-prod\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  project: shop\n  source:\n    repoURL: https://source.example.com/platform/shop-deploy\n    targetRevision: main\n    path: overlays/prod\n  destination:\n    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n    namespace: shop\n  syncPolicy:\n    automated:\n      prune: true\n      selfHeal: true\n    syncOptions:\n      - CreateNamespace=true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Application\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api-prod\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argocd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">project:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repoURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://source.example.com/platform/shop-deploy\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRevision:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">overlays/prod\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncPolicy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">automated:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prune:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selfHeal:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncOptions:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CreateNamespace=true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Reading it: the controller watches the \u003ccode>overlays/prod\u003c/code> path of the configuration repository and keeps the \u003ccode>shop\u003c/code> namespace identical to it. \u003ccode>prune: true\u003c/code> deletes objects removed from configuration. \u003ccode>selfHeal: true\u003c/code> reverts manual changes made in the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-tip\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Tip\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use automated sync for development and staging. For production, many teams keep \u003ccode>selfHeal\u003c/code> on but require a manual sync, or an approval, so a person decides when the change goes live.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"everyday-operations\">Everyday operations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app list\nargocd app diff shop-api-prod\nargocd app sync shop-api-prod\nargocd app history shop-api-prod\nargocd app rollback shop-api-prod 12\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            CLUSTER                         NAMESPACE  STATUS     HEALTH\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api-prod   https://kubernetes.default.svc  shop       Synced     Healthy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app diff shop-api-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sync\u003c/span> shop-api-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> shop-api-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app rollback shop-api-prod 12\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rolling back by reverting the configuration change is better than the \u003ccode>rollback\u003c/code> command, because it keeps the stored configuration and the cluster in agreement. Where you used \u003ccode>rollback\u003c/code> in an emergency, record the same change in configuration afterward, or the controller will push the bad version again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets\">Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You cannot store plain Secret manifests in a shared configuration repository. Use one of these approaches:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>External secret management\u003c/strong>: an operator reads values from your cloud secret manager and creates the Kubernetes Secret\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sealed or encrypted secrets\u003c/strong>: the stored value is encrypted and only the cluster can decrypt it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-controller-keeps-undoing-an-urgent-fix\">Incident: the controller keeps undoing an urgent fix\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an outage you scale the deployment to 10 replicas with \u003ccode>kubectl scale\u003c/code>. A minute later it is back at 3, and the site is still struggling.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl scale deployment/shop-api -n shop --replicas=10\nkubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl scale deployment/shop-api -n shop --replicas=10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME       READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api   3/3     3            3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>selfHeal\u003c/code> is working as designed: the configuration says 3, so the controller reverted your manual change. In an emergency you have two honest options. Make the change the intended way, by editing the replica count in the configuration and letting the controller apply it, which takes a minute but leaves a reviewable record. Or use the documented break-glass step of pausing automated sync for that application, scaling manually, and re-enabling sync when the incident is over. Either way, finish by making the configuration match what you decided, and review whether the replica count belongs under an autoscaler instead of a fixed number.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Storing real secrets in the configuration repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting application commits trigger production changes directly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turning off \u003ccode>selfHeal\u003c/code> permanently and returning to drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making emergency changes by hand and never recording them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"push-versus-pull","text":"Push versus pull"},{"id":"the-flow","text":"The flow"},{"id":"argo-cd-and-flux","text":"Argo CD and Flux"},{"id":"everyday-operations","text":"Everyday operations"},{"id":"secrets","text":"Secrets"},{"id":"incident-the-controller-keeps-undoing-an-urgent-fix","text":"Incident: the controller keeps undoing an urgent fix"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"Flux documentation","u":"https://fluxcd.io/flux/"},{"t":"Argo Rollouts documentation","u":"https://argo-rollouts.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"In a push pipeline, your CI system holds credentials to the cluster and runs kubectl apply . That works, but those credentials are a target, nobody notices when someone changes the cluster by hand, and the real state of production lives in the memory of whoever ran the last command. Declarative delivery reverses the direction: a controller inside the cluster pulls the desired configuration from a reviewed source and keeps the cluster matching it. What you will learn The pull model and its benefits How Argo CD and Flux work An Argo CD Application Sync, drift correction and rollback Push versus pull Push (pipeline applies) Pull (controller applies) Cluster credentials Held by the CI system Stay inside the cluster Manual changes Remain until the next deploy, unnoticed Detected and reverted or reported Source of truth The last pipeline run The reviewed configuration Audit trail CI logs Change history of the configuration Rollback Re-run an older pipeline Revert the change; the controller reconciles This is the same reconcile loop you met in the first Kubernetes lesson, applied to deployment itself . The flow CI builds and tests the application and publishes an image with a unique tag. A change to the configuration repository updates the image tag for an environment. This is a normal reviewed change. The controller notices the new desired state, compares it with the cluster and applies the difference. It reports Synced and Healthy , or shows exactly what differs. Keep application source and deployment configuration in separate repositories when you can. That stops every code commit from being a deploy request, and lets a platform team control environments without touching application code. Argo CD and Flux Both are widely used, CNCF-hosted controllers for this model. Argo CD Flux Style Application-centric with a web UI and CLI Set of controllers driven by Kubernetes objects Strength Visual view of sync and health; easy to explore Composable and lightweight; strong automation of image updates Config unit Application A source object, Kustomization , HelmRelease Choose either; the concepts are identical. The example below uses Argo CD. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Application metadata: name: shop-api-prod namespace: argocd spec: project: shop source: repoURL: https://source.example.com/platform/shop-deploy targetRevision: main path: overlays/prod destination: server: https://kubernetes.default.svc namespace: shop syncPolicy: automated: prune: true selfHeal: true syncOptions: - CreateNamespace=true Reading it: the controller watches the overlays/prod path of the configuration repository and keeps the shop namespace identical to it. prune: true deletes objects removed from configuration. selfHeal: true reverts manual changes made in the cluster. Tip Use automated sync for development and staging. For production, many teams keep selfHeal on but require a manual sync, or an approval, so a person decides when the change goes live. Everyday operations $ argocd app list NAME CLUSTER NAMESPACE STATUS HEALTH shop-api-prod https://kubernetes.default.svc shop Synced Healthy $ argocd app diff shop-api-prod $ argocd app sync shop-api-prod $ argocd app history shop-api-prod $ argocd app rollback shop-api-prod 12 Rolling back by reverting the configuration change is better than the rollback command, because it keeps the stored configuration and the cluster in agreement. Where you used rollback in an emergency, record the same change in configuration afterward, or the controller will push the bad version again. Secrets You cannot store plain Secret manifests in a shared configuration repository. Use one of these approaches: External secret management : an operator reads values from your cloud secret manager and creates the Kubernetes Secret Sealed or encrypted secrets : the stored value is encrypted and only the cluster can decrypt it Incident: the controller keeps undoing an urgent fix Incident During an outage you scale the deployment to "},"cicd/release-management-and-feature-flags":{"html":"\u003cp>Early pipelines treat a release as one event: the code is deployed and users see it. Mature teams separate the two. They deploy code often and in small pieces, and they release a feature to users later, gradually, and with a switch that can turn it off in seconds. This reduces fear, shortens lead time and makes rollbacks boring.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between deploying and releasing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Types of feature flags and their lifecycle\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Environments, approvals and promotion\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Progressive delivery: percentages, rings and kill switches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Flag debt and how to avoid it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deploy-is-not-release\">Deploy is not release\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Deploying puts a new version on servers. Releasing makes a behaviour visible to users. With a flag, you can deploy code with the feature switched off, test it in production with staff, enable it for 1 percent of users, then 10 percent, then all. If something is wrong, you turn the flag off; no redeploy and no rollback of unrelated changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"if flags.enabled(&quot;new-checkout&quot;, user=user.id, default=False):\n    return new_checkout(cart)\nreturn old_checkout(cart)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> flags.enabled(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new-checkout&quot;\u003c/span>, user=user.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>, default=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> new_checkout(cart)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> old_checkout(cart)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>default=False\u003c/code> matters: if the flag service is down, the code must fall back to a safe behaviour, not fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kinds-of-flags\">Kinds of flags\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Lifetime\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Release flag\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hide unfinished or risky work\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Days to weeks, then delete\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Experiment flag\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Compare variants\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Until the result is known\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Operational flag (kill switch)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Turn off an expensive or risky path in an incident\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Long\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Permission flag\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Enable for a plan or customer\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Long, part of the product\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each flag needs an owner, a purpose and a removal date. Old release flags leave dead code and untested combinations, which is called flag debt. Review flags regularly and delete them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"environments-and-promotion\">Environments and promotion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep environments few and similar: development, staging (production-like), production. Promote the same built artifact through them, and change only configuration. Do not rebuild for each environment. Approvals should match risk: automatic for low-risk changes with good tests; a human check for changes to payments or data schemas. Record who approved what, and when.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"progressive-delivery\">Progressive delivery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Canary:\u003c/strong> send a small share of traffic to the new version and watch error rate and latency.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Rings:\u003c/strong> release to internal users, then a small group, then everyone.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Automatic rollback:\u003c/strong> if a metric crosses a threshold during rollout, the system stops or reverts without a human.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Percentage rollouts by stable hash:\u003c/strong> hash the user id, so a user keeps the same experience.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"versions-and-rollbacks\">Versions and rollbacks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use semantic versions or build ids that map exactly to a commit. Keep the previous version ready, and make database changes backwards compatible (add a column first, switch code, remove the old column later) so a rollback of code is always possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A release changes a database column name and the code at the same time. Why does a rollback fail?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The old code expects the old column name, which no longer exists, so rolling back the code breaks it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use an expand and contract approach: add the new column, write to both, switch reads, and remove the old column in a later release.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-feature-that-could-not-be-switched-off\">Incident: the feature that could not be switched off\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new pricing calculation shows wrong totals to 30 percent of customers after a release, and turning it off takes two hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"flagctl list --stale\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>flagctl list --stale\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">new-pricing       owner: pay-team   created: 2026-02-10   100% on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">legacy-discount   owner: (none)     created: 2024-06-01   100% on\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The change shipped without a flag, so the only remedy was a rollback that also removed other fixes. Afterwards the team required a flag for risky behaviour changes, added a kill switch for pricing, ran a canary with an automatic abort on error rate, and set a monthly flag clean-up.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No safe default when the flag service fails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Flags without owners or removal dates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the artifact for each environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Database changes that block rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"deploy-is-not-release","text":"Deploy is not release"},{"id":"kinds-of-flags","text":"Kinds of flags"},{"id":"environments-and-promotion","text":"Environments and promotion"},{"id":"progressive-delivery","text":"Progressive delivery"},{"id":"versions-and-rollbacks","text":"Versions and rollbacks"},{"id":"incident-the-feature-that-could-not-be-switched-off","text":"Incident: the feature that could not be switched off"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenFeature","u":"https://openfeature.dev/"},{"t":"Continuous Delivery","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"}],"text":"Early pipelines treat a release as one event: the code is deployed and users see it. Mature teams separate the two. They deploy code often and in small pieces, and they release a feature to users later, gradually, and with a switch that can turn it off in seconds. This reduces fear, shortens lead time and makes rollbacks boring. What you will learn The difference between deploying and releasing Types of feature flags and their lifecycle Environments, approvals and promotion Progressive delivery: percentages, rings and kill switches Flag debt and how to avoid it Deploy is not release Deploying puts a new version on servers. Releasing makes a behaviour visible to users. With a flag, you can deploy code with the feature switched off, test it in production with staff, enable it for 1 percent of users, then 10 percent, then all. If something is wrong, you turn the flag off; no redeploy and no rollback of unrelated changes. if flags.enabled( \"new-checkout\" , user=user. id , default= False ): return new_checkout(cart) return old_checkout(cart) The default=False matters: if the flag service is down, the code must fall back to a safe behaviour, not fail. Kinds of flags Kind Purpose Lifetime Release flag Hide unfinished or risky work Days to weeks, then delete Experiment flag Compare variants Until the result is known Operational flag (kill switch) Turn off an expensive or risky path in an incident Long Permission flag Enable for a plan or customer Long, part of the product Each flag needs an owner, a purpose and a removal date. Old release flags leave dead code and untested combinations, which is called flag debt. Review flags regularly and delete them. Environments and promotion Keep environments few and similar: development, staging (production-like), production. Promote the same built artifact through them, and change only configuration. Do not rebuild for each environment. Approvals should match risk: automatic for low-risk changes with good tests; a human check for changes to payments or data schemas. Record who approved what, and when. Progressive delivery Canary: send a small share of traffic to the new version and watch error rate and latency. Rings: release to internal users, then a small group, then everyone. Automatic rollback: if a metric crosses a threshold during rollout, the system stops or reverts without a human. Percentage rollouts by stable hash: hash the user id, so a user keeps the same experience. Versions and rollbacks Use semantic versions or build ids that map exactly to a commit. Keep the previous version ready, and make database changes backwards compatible (add a column first, switch code, remove the old column later) so a rollback of code is always possible. Predict first. A release changes a database column name and the code at the same time. Why does a rollback fail? Your guess Answer: The old code expects the old column name, which no longer exists, so rolling back the code breaks it. Use an expand and contract approach: add the new column, write to both, switch reads, and remove the old column in a later release. Incident: the feature that could not be switched off Incident A new pricing calculation shows wrong totals to 30 percent of customers after a release, and turning it off takes two hours. $ flagctl list --stale new-pricing owner: pay-team created: 2026-02-10 100% on legacy-discount owner: (none) created: 2024-06-01 100% on The change shipped without a flag, so the only remedy was a rollback that also removed other fixes. Afterwards the team required a flag for risky behaviour changes, added a kill switch for pricing, ran a canary with an automatic abort on error rate, and set a monthly flag clean-up. Common mistakes No safe default when the flag service fails Flags without owners or removal dates Rebuilding the artifact for each environment Database changes that block rollback"},"cicd/fast-pipelines-caching-parallelism-monorepos":{"html":"\u003cp>A pipeline that takes forty minutes teaches people to batch changes, skip tests and stop trusting the result. Fast feedback is a quality feature. The good news is that most slow pipelines waste time in a few predictable places, and you can fix them one by one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Measuring a pipeline before changing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parallel jobs and test sharding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dependency and build caches that are correct\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running only what changed in a monorepo\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Runner sizing and keeping images small\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure-first\">Measure first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write down the duration of each stage over the last twenty runs. Look at the median and at the slowest run, not the best. The usual suspects: installing dependencies, building images from scratch, a long single test job, waiting for a shared runner, and slow end-to-end tests. Fix the biggest first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"parallelism\">Parallelism\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Jobs that do not depend on each other should run at the same time: lint, unit tests, type checks, and security scans. Split a long test job into shards (for example four jobs, each running a quarter of the tests, balanced by recorded duration):\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"test:\n  parallel: 4\n  script:\n    - ./run-tests.sh --shard $SHARD_INDEX/$SHARD_TOTAL\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">parallel:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./run-tests.sh\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--shard\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$SHARD_INDEX/$SHARD_TOTAL\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Keep the number of parallel jobs below your runner capacity, or jobs wait in a queue and the gain disappears.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caching-correctly\">Caching correctly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cache must be fast and must never serve stale or poisoned data. Rules:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Key dependency caches on a hash of the lockfile, so a change in dependencies creates a new cache.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache the package manager&#39;s download directory, not the installed project.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use read-only caches for pull request builds when you worry about cache poisoning from untrusted code.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For container builds, use a registry cache (see the lesson on BuildKit).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build tools such as Bazel, Gradle and Nx have remote caches that reuse build and test results by input hash.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"monorepos-only-run-what-changed\">Monorepos: only run what changed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In a repository with many services, build and test only what a change can affect:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rules:\n  - changes: [&quot;services/payments/**/*&quot;, &quot;libs/common/**/*&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">changes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;services/payments/**/*&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;libs/common/**/*&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Dependency-aware tools (Nx, Bazel, Turborepo) compute the affected targets from the dependency graph. Be careful: if a shared library changes, all dependents must run. Always run the full pipeline on the main branch on a schedule to catch gaps in the rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"other-savings\">Other savings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use a small, prebuilt image with your tools instead of installing them in each run.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run slow end-to-end tests after merge or on a schedule, and keep a small smoke test on each change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cancel old runs when a new commit arrives for the same branch.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use larger runners for heavy builds; one four-core job may be cheaper than a sixteen-minute two-core job.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fail fast: run the cheapest checks first.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your dependency cache key is only the branch name. What goes wrong?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> When the lockfile changes, the old cache is reused, so builds use outdated dependencies, or fail in confusing ways.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Include a hash of the lockfile in the key.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-thirty-eight-minute-pipeline\">Incident: the thirty-eight minute pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team&#39;s merge request pipeline grows to 38 minutes, and developers start merging without waiting for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">stage        median   p95\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">install      9m       14m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">unit tests   16m      19m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">e2e          10m      22m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">build image  3m       5m\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Dependencies were reinstalled each run, the unit tests ran in one job and the end-to-end tests ran on every change. The team cached by lockfile hash (install fell to one minute), sharded unit tests into four jobs (to five minutes), moved most end-to-end tests to run after merge, kept three smoke tests on each change, and canceled outdated runs. The median fell to eleven minutes, and people started to wait for it again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Optimising without measuring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caches that can be poisoned or stale\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>More parallel jobs than runners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the full run for the monorepo\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"measure-first","text":"Measure first"},{"id":"parallelism","text":"Parallelism"},{"id":"caching-correctly","text":"Caching correctly"},{"id":"monorepos-only-run-what-changed","text":"Monorepos: only run what changed"},{"id":"other-savings","text":"Other savings"},{"id":"incident-the-thirty-eight-minute-pipeline","text":"Incident: the thirty-eight minute pipeline"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Continuous Delivery: Deployment pipeline","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/implementing/patterns/"},{"t":"DORA research","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"A pipeline that takes forty minutes teaches people to batch changes, skip tests and stop trusting the result. Fast feedback is a quality feature. The good news is that most slow pipelines waste time in a few predictable places, and you can fix them one by one. What you will learn Measuring a pipeline before changing it Parallel jobs and test sharding Dependency and build caches that are correct Running only what changed in a monorepo Runner sizing and keeping images small Measure first Write down the duration of each stage over the last twenty runs. Look at the median and at the slowest run, not the best. The usual suspects: installing dependencies, building images from scratch, a long single test job, waiting for a shared runner, and slow end-to-end tests. Fix the biggest first. Parallelism Jobs that do not depend on each other should run at the same time: lint, unit tests, type checks, and security scans. Split a long test job into shards (for example four jobs, each running a quarter of the tests, balanced by recorded duration): test: parallel: 4 script: - ./run-tests.sh --shard $SHARD_INDEX/$SHARD_TOTAL Keep the number of parallel jobs below your runner capacity, or jobs wait in a queue and the gain disappears. Caching correctly A cache must be fast and must never serve stale or poisoned data. Rules: Key dependency caches on a hash of the lockfile, so a change in dependencies creates a new cache. Cache the package manager&#39;s download directory, not the installed project. Use read-only caches for pull request builds when you worry about cache poisoning from untrusted code. For container builds, use a registry cache (see the lesson on BuildKit). Build tools such as Bazel, Gradle and Nx have remote caches that reuse build and test results by input hash. Monorepos: only run what changed In a repository with many services, build and test only what a change can affect: rules: - changes: [ \"services/payments/**/*\" , \"libs/common/**/*\" ] Dependency-aware tools (Nx, Bazel, Turborepo) compute the affected targets from the dependency graph. Be careful: if a shared library changes, all dependents must run. Always run the full pipeline on the main branch on a schedule to catch gaps in the rules. Other savings Use a small, prebuilt image with your tools instead of installing them in each run. Run slow end-to-end tests after merge or on a schedule, and keep a small smoke test on each change. Cancel old runs when a new commit arrives for the same branch. Use larger runners for heavy builds; one four-core job may be cheaper than a sixteen-minute two-core job. Fail fast: run the cheapest checks first. Predict first. Your dependency cache key is only the branch name. What goes wrong? Your guess Answer: When the lockfile changes, the old cache is reused, so builds use outdated dependencies, or fail in confusing ways. Include a hash of the lockfile in the key. Incident: the thirty-eight minute pipeline Incident A team&#39;s merge request pipeline grows to 38 minutes, and developers start merging without waiting for it. stage median p95 install 9m 14m unit tests 16m 19m e2e 10m 22m build image 3m 5m Dependencies were reinstalled each run, the unit tests ran in one job and the end-to-end tests ran on every change. The team cached by lockfile hash (install fell to one minute), sharded unit tests into four jobs (to five minutes), moved most end-to-end tests to run after merge, kept three smoke tests on each change, and canceled outdated runs. The median fell to eleven minutes, and people started to wait for it again. Common mistakes Optimising without measuring Caches that can be poisoned or stale More parallel jobs than runners Skipping the full run for the monorepo"},"cicd/reusable-pipelines-and-templates":{"html":"\u003cp>Forty services each have their own copy of a build pipeline. When the security team needs a new scan step, forty files have to change, and thirty-one of them differ slightly. Reusable pipelines solve this the same way libraries solve repeated code: write once, version, and let teams call it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why copy and paste pipelines drift and hurt\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared templates and reusable workflows with inputs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning shared pipeline code so changes are safe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guard rails: what to enforce centrally and what to leave to teams\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cost-of-copied-pipelines\">The cost of copied pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Copying a pipeline looks free on day one. Over time the copies diverge: one service pins a tool version, another does not, a third skips the scan &quot;temporarily&quot; and never turns it back on. When a fix is needed, you must find every copy, and each one needs its own review. Inconsistent pipelines also make audits hard, because you cannot say &quot;all services run a vulnerability scan&quot; with confidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templates-with-inputs\">Templates with inputs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most CI systems let one pipeline include another and pass inputs. The details differ, but the shape is the same: a central definition, and a short file in each service that says what to build.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# in the service repository\ninclude: shared/pipelines/build-and-test.yml@v3\nvariables:\n  LANGUAGE: python\n  PYTHON_VERSION: &quot;3.12&quot;\n  DEPLOY_TARGET: staging\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># in the service repository\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">include:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shared/pipelines/build-and-test.yml@v3\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">variables:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">LANGUAGE:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">PYTHON_VERSION:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;3.12&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">DEPLOY_TARGET:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">staging\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The shared template contains the steps: checkout, cache, build, test, scan, publish. Inputs let a team choose the language or the target, but they cannot remove the scan step. Keep inputs few and typed. A template with forty options is a configuration language nobody can read.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"version-the-shared-code\">Version the shared code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A change to a shared pipeline affects every user at once, so treat it like a library release. Tag versions (\u003ccode>v3\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>v3.2.1\u003c/code>), publish release notes, and let teams pin a major version. Test the template itself on a sample project before you tag it. Roll out breaking changes as a new major version, and give teams time and a migration note to move.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A safe pattern is \u003cem>canary then fleet\u003c/em>: move two or three friendly services to the new version, watch their runs for a week, and then update the default.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guard-rails-not-handcuffs\">Guard rails, not handcuffs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Decide what must be the same everywhere: the security scan, artifact signing, the rule that production deploys need a reviewed commit on the main branch, and the audit log. Make those parts of the template, or enforce them with a required check on every repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Leave the rest to teams: extra test stages, extra environments, and tools specific to their language. If teams must fight the template to do their job, they will fork it, and you are back to copies. Measure adoption, for example the share of pipelines on the current version, and treat it as a service you run for the other teams.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A shared template adds a new mandatory scan, and 12 pipelines fail on their next run. How could the rollout have been safer?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Release it as a new version, test on a few canary services, report findings without failing for a trial period, then make it required with notice.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Shared changes need versioning, canaries and a soft launch.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-edit-forty-broken-builds\">Incident: One edit, forty broken builds\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A platform engineer renamed a variable in the shared template. Within an hour, forty services could not deploy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ci run --pipeline checkout --branch main\ngrep -c &quot;@main&quot; services/*/ci.yml | grep -v &quot;:0&quot; | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ci run --pipeline checkout --branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: variable DEPLOY_ENV is not defined (template build-and-test.yml@main)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;@main&quot;\u003c/span> services/*/ci.yml | grep -v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:0&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">40\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every service referred to the template by the moving name \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>, so the change reached all of them immediately. The fix was to revert the rename and to require version tags in \u003ccode>include\u003c/code> lines, with a lint check that rejects \u003ccode>@main\u003c/code>. Breaking changes now go out as a new major version, and a weekly report shows how many pipelines still use each version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Referring to a template by a moving branch name\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the template dozens of options\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making every team copy the file because the shared one is hard to change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enforcing nothing centrally, so the scan quietly disappears from some services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-cost-of-copied-pipelines","text":"The cost of copied pipelines"},{"id":"templates-with-inputs","text":"Templates with inputs"},{"id":"version-the-shared-code","text":"Version the shared code"},{"id":"guard-rails-not-handcuffs","text":"Guard rails, not handcuffs"},{"id":"incident-one-edit-forty-broken-builds","text":"Incident: One edit, forty broken builds"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Continuous Delivery by Jez Humble and Dave Farley","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"},{"t":"The DORA research programme","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"Forty services each have their own copy of a build pipeline. When the security team needs a new scan step, forty files have to change, and thirty-one of them differ slightly. Reusable pipelines solve this the same way libraries solve repeated code: write once, version, and let teams call it. What you will learn Why copy and paste pipelines drift and hurt Shared templates and reusable workflows with inputs Versioning shared pipeline code so changes are safe Guard rails: what to enforce centrally and what to leave to teams The cost of copied pipelines Copying a pipeline looks free on day one. Over time the copies diverge: one service pins a tool version, another does not, a third skips the scan \"temporarily\" and never turns it back on. When a fix is needed, you must find every copy, and each one needs its own review. Inconsistent pipelines also make audits hard, because you cannot say \"all services run a vulnerability scan\" with confidence. Templates with inputs Most CI systems let one pipeline include another and pass inputs. The details differ, but the shape is the same: a central definition, and a short file in each service that says what to build. # in the service repository include: shared/pipelines/build-and-test.yml@v3 variables: LANGUAGE: python PYTHON_VERSION: \"3.12\" DEPLOY_TARGET: staging The shared template contains the steps: checkout, cache, build, test, scan, publish. Inputs let a team choose the language or the target, but they cannot remove the scan step. Keep inputs few and typed. A template with forty options is a configuration language nobody can read. Version the shared code A change to a shared pipeline affects every user at once, so treat it like a library release. Tag versions ( v3 , v3.2.1 ), publish release notes, and let teams pin a major version. Test the template itself on a sample project before you tag it. Roll out breaking changes as a new major version, and give teams time and a migration note to move. A safe pattern is canary then fleet : move two or three friendly services to the new version, watch their runs for a week, and then update the default. Guard rails, not handcuffs Decide what must be the same everywhere: the security scan, artifact signing, the rule that production deploys need a reviewed commit on the main branch, and the audit log. Make those parts of the template, or enforce them with a required check on every repository. Leave the rest to teams: extra test stages, extra environments, and tools specific to their language. If teams must fight the template to do their job, they will fork it, and you are back to copies. Measure adoption, for example the share of pipelines on the current version, and treat it as a service you run for the other teams. Predict first. A shared template adds a new mandatory scan, and 12 pipelines fail on their next run. How could the rollout have been safer? Your guess Answer: Release it as a new version, test on a few canary services, report findings without failing for a trial period, then make it required with notice. Shared changes need versioning, canaries and a soft launch. Incident: One edit, forty broken builds Incident A platform engineer renamed a variable in the shared template. Within an hour, forty services could not deploy. $ ci run --pipeline checkout --branch main error: variable DEPLOY_ENV is not defined (template build-and-test.yml@main) $ grep -c \"@main\" services/*/ci.yml | grep -v \":0\" | wc -l 40 Every service referred to the template by the moving name main , so the change reached all of them immediately. The fix was to revert the rename and to require version tags in include lines, with a lint check that rejects @main . Breaking changes now go out as a new major version, and a weekly report shows how many pipelines still use each version. Common mistakes Referring to a template by a moving branch name Giving the template dozens of options Making every team copy the file because the shared one is hard to change Enforcing nothing centr"},"cicd/environments-promotion-and-approvals":{"html":"\u003cp>The release checklist says &quot;deploy to staging, test, deploy to production&quot;, but each deploy rebuilds the code and picks up slightly different dependencies. What passed in staging is not exactly what reaches production. Promotion fixes this: build once, then move the same artifact through environments, with a gate at each step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What an environment is and how many you need\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automatic gates and human approvals, and when to use each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Records: who approved what, and what is running where\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"environments-and-their-purpose\">Environments and their purpose\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An environment is a place where the application runs with its own configuration and data. Common ones are \u003cstrong>dev\u003c/strong> (fast, breakable), \u003cstrong>test\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>staging\u003c/strong> (production-like, used for final checks) and \u003cstrong>production\u003c/strong>. Add more only when a stage has a real purpose, such as performance testing or a customer demo. Each extra environment costs money and attention, and an environment that drifts from production gives false confidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Keep differences between environments in configuration (addresses, sizes, secrets), not in code, and keep configuration in files that are reviewed like code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-once-promote-many-times\">Build once, promote many times\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The pipeline builds an artifact (a container image or package) one time, gives it a version, and records its digest. Every later stage deploys exactly that artifact. Only the configuration changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"build  -&gt;  image shop@sha256:9f2c...  -&gt;  dev  -&gt;  staging  -&gt;  production\n                                          ^ tests    ^ checks     ^ approval\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">build  -&gt;  image shop@sha256:9f2c...  -&gt;  dev  -&gt;  staging  -&gt;  production\n                                          ^ tests    ^ checks     ^ approval\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If a bug is found in staging, you fix it in code, build a new artifact, and start the journey again. You never patch an artifact on its way to production. Promotion by digest also gives you rollback for free: the previous digest is known and was already tested.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gates-automatic-and-human\">Gates: automatic and human\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>gate\u003c/strong> is a condition for moving to the next environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Automatic gates: tests pass, scan has no critical findings, smoke test on the new environment, error rate normal for ten minutes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Human approvals: needed where a person must judge risk, for example a database migration or a change in a regulated system.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Use automation wherever the answer can be computed, because people approving everything become a rubber stamp. When a human approves, show the evidence: what changed, test results, the risk, and the rollback plan. The approver should not be the author. Also define an emergency path with fewer steps but the same record, so that urgent fixes do not tempt people to bypass the pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"know-what-runs-where\">Know what runs where\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>At any moment you should be able to answer: which version runs in production, who approved it, and which commit it came from. Record deploy events (environment, version, time, approver) in one place, and show the current versions on a dashboard. Add a deploy marker to monitoring graphs, so that a change in error rate can be tied to a release within seconds. For audits, keep the approval and test evidence with the release for the period the rules require.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Staging uses a build from Monday, but production gets a build from Tuesday made from the same branch. What risk did you create?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Production runs code and dependencies that were never tested in staging. Promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Rebuilding for each environment breaks the link between test and release.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-tested-in-staging-broken-in-production\">Incident: Tested in staging, broken in production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A release passed every staging check and failed on its first request in production with a missing library.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker inspect --format &quot;{{.Image}}&quot; staging-shop prod-shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{{.Image}}&quot;\u003c/span> staging-shop prod-shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sha256:41aa...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sha256:b072...\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Staging and production ran different images. The pipeline had one job per environment and each job ran its own \u003ccode>docker build\u003c/code>. A transitive dependency was released between the two builds. The team changed the pipeline to build once, push the image with its digest, and deploy that digest to each environment, and a check now fails the production deploy if the digest was not first deployed and tested in staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the artifact for each environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting staging drift away from production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Approvals without evidence, so people click through\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No emergency path, which leads to bypassing the pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"environments-and-their-purpose","text":"Environments and their purpose"},{"id":"build-once-promote-many-times","text":"Build once, promote many times"},{"id":"gates-automatic-and-human","text":"Gates: automatic and human"},{"id":"know-what-runs-where","text":"Know what runs where"},{"id":"incident-tested-in-staging-broken-in-production","text":"Incident: Tested in staging, broken in production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Continuous Delivery","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"},{"t":"Twelve-Factor App: Config","u":"https://12factor.net/config"}],"text":"The release checklist says \"deploy to staging, test, deploy to production\", but each deploy rebuilds the code and picks up slightly different dependencies. What passed in staging is not exactly what reaches production. Promotion fixes this: build once, then move the same artifact through environments, with a gate at each step. What you will learn What an environment is and how many you need Promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding Automatic gates and human approvals, and when to use each Records: who approved what, and what is running where Environments and their purpose An environment is a place where the application runs with its own configuration and data. Common ones are dev (fast, breakable), test or staging (production-like, used for final checks) and production . Add more only when a stage has a real purpose, such as performance testing or a customer demo. Each extra environment costs money and attention, and an environment that drifts from production gives false confidence. Keep differences between environments in configuration (addresses, sizes, secrets), not in code, and keep configuration in files that are reviewed like code. Build once, promote many times The pipeline builds an artifact (a container image or package) one time, gives it a version, and records its digest. Every later stage deploys exactly that artifact. Only the configuration changes. build -> image shop@sha256:9f2c... -> dev -> staging -> production ^ tests ^ checks ^ approval If a bug is found in staging, you fix it in code, build a new artifact, and start the journey again. You never patch an artifact on its way to production. Promotion by digest also gives you rollback for free: the previous digest is known and was already tested. Gates: automatic and human A gate is a condition for moving to the next environment. Automatic gates: tests pass, scan has no critical findings, smoke test on the new environment, error rate normal for ten minutes. Human approvals: needed where a person must judge risk, for example a database migration or a change in a regulated system. Use automation wherever the answer can be computed, because people approving everything become a rubber stamp. When a human approves, show the evidence: what changed, test results, the risk, and the rollback plan. The approver should not be the author. Also define an emergency path with fewer steps but the same record, so that urgent fixes do not tempt people to bypass the pipeline. Know what runs where At any moment you should be able to answer: which version runs in production, who approved it, and which commit it came from. Record deploy events (environment, version, time, approver) in one place, and show the current versions on a dashboard. Add a deploy marker to monitoring graphs, so that a change in error rate can be tied to a release within seconds. For audits, keep the approval and test evidence with the release for the period the rules require. Predict first. Staging uses a build from Monday, but production gets a build from Tuesday made from the same branch. What risk did you create? Your guess Answer: Production runs code and dependencies that were never tested in staging. Promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding. Rebuilding for each environment breaks the link between test and release. Incident: Tested in staging, broken in production Incident A release passed every staging check and failed on its first request in production with a missing library. $ docker inspect --format \"{{.Image}}\" staging-shop prod-shop sha256:41aa... sha256:b072... Staging and production ran different images. The pipeline had one job per environment and each job ran its own docker build . A transitive dependency was released between the two builds. The team changed the pipeline to build once, push the image with its digest, and deploy that digest to each environment, and a check now fails the production deploy if the digest was not first deployed and tested in staging. Common mistakes Reb"},"cicd/debugging-pipelines":{"html":"\u003cp>Pipelines fail for reasons that have nothing to do with your change: a cache that went stale, a dependency that moved, a runner that ran out of disk, or a test that depends on timing. If you cannot tell a real failure from noise, people start re-running until green, and real problems pass through.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading a failing job from the first error, not the last line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reproducing a failure outside the pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Flaky tests, cache problems and drift between local and CI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging secrets and permissions without printing them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-first-error\">Read the first error\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The last lines of a job often say only &quot;exit code 1&quot;. The cause is higher up. Search the log for the first line containing \u003ccode>error\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>failed\u003c/code> or a stack trace, and check what ran just before it. Note the exact command, the working directory and the versions of tools printed at the start of the job.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reproduce-it-locally\">Reproduce it locally\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run --rm -it -v &quot;$PWD&quot;:/work -w /work node:20.11 bash   # same image as the job\nnpm ci &amp;&amp; npm test                                                # same commands\nenv | sort | grep -v -i -E 'token|secret|key|pass'                # compare environment, safely\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it -v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$PWD\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>:/work -w /work node:20.11 bash   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># same image as the job\u003c/span>\nnpm ci &amp;&amp; npm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span>                                                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># same commands\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | grep -v -i -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;token|secret|key|pass&#x27;\u003c/span>                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># compare environment, safely\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use the same image tag, the same commands and a clean checkout. If it passes locally and fails in CI, compare tool versions, environment variables, file permissions, time zone, available memory and network access.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"flaky-or-real\">Flaky or real?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Sign\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Likely cause\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Fix\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Passes on retry, fails randomly\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Timing, order or shared state\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Remove sleeps, isolate data, fix the test\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fails only on one runner\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runner disk, memory or stale cache\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Clean the runner, pin its image\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fails after a dependency release\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Unpinned versions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Use a lockfile and pinned versions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fails near midnight or month end\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Date logic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Inject the clock in tests\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Track flaky tests in a list with a count. A retry hides the symptom and keeps the cost, so use it only with a limit and a ticket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caches-and-secrets\">Caches and secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cache keyed only on the branch name can serve old build output. Key it on a hash of the lockfile. For secrets, check that the variable exists and is not empty without printing it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[ -n &quot;${API_TOKEN:-}&quot; ] &amp;&amp; echo &quot;API_TOKEN is set (length ${#API_TOKEN})&quot; || echo &quot;API_TOKEN missing&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">[ -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${API_TOKEN:-}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ] &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;API_TOKEN is set (length \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${#API_TOKEN}\u003c/span>)&quot;\u003c/span> || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;API_TOKEN missing&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Masking hides values in logs but does not stop a script from encoding and printing them, so never enable shell tracing in jobs that handle secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A test passes alone but fails when the whole suite runs. What is the most likely reason?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Tests share state such as a database row, a file or a global variable, and one test changes what another expects.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Run the failing test with the one before it, then isolate or reset the shared data. Random test ordering finds these problems early.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"first&gt;repro;repro&gt;classify;repro&gt;env;classify&gt;fix;env&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Triage of a red pipeline\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"first\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">First error in the log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"repro\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reproduce locally\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"classify\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Real or flaky\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"env\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Environment drift\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and prevent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, First error in the log to Reproduce locally; 2, Reproduce locally to Real or flaky; 3, Reproduce locally to Environment drift; 4, Real or flaky to Fix and prevent; 5, Environment drift to Fix and prevent.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"first\">\u003cdt>First error in the log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Find the earliest error, the exact command and tool versions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The last line usually only repeats the exit code.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"repro\">\u003cdt>Reproduce locally \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run the same image and commands on a clean checkout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A different local setup passes and proves nothing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"classify\">\u003cdt>Real or flaky \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Retry once and compare with recent runs on the same commit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Endless retries hide a genuine defect.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"env\">\u003cdt>Environment drift \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare versions, variables, caches and runner state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unpinned tools change without any commit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix and prevent \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add a pin, a test or a cache key so it cannot recur.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A one-time rerun leaves the cause in place.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-green-yesterday-red-today-same-commit\">Incident: green yesterday, red today, same commit\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The main branch pipeline fails at the build step with a compile error. No one changed the code since yesterday.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"diff &lt;(grep -A2 'lodash' yesterday.log) &lt;(grep -A2 'lodash' today.log)\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>diff &lt;(grep -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;lodash&#x27;\u003c/span> yesterday.log) &lt;(grep -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;lodash&#x27;\u003c/span> today.log)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt; lodash 4.17.21\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&gt; lodash 4.18.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The project installed dependencies with a range and no lockfile, so a new release was picked up silently and broke the build. The team committed a lockfile, switched to \u003ccode>npm ci\u003c/code>, pinned the build image, and added a scheduled build so drift shows up before a release day.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Re-running until green\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only the last 20 lines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caches keyed on a branch name\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Printing environment variables that contain secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"read-the-first-error","text":"Read the first error"},{"id":"reproduce-it-locally","text":"Reproduce it locally"},{"id":"flaky-or-real","text":"Flaky or real?"},{"id":"caches-and-secrets","text":"Caches and secrets"},{"id":"incident-green-yesterday-red-today-same-commit","text":"Incident: green yesterday, red today, same commit"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: Testing for Reliability","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/testing-reliability/"},{"t":"Continuous Delivery","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"}],"text":"Pipelines fail for reasons that have nothing to do with your change: a cache that went stale, a dependency that moved, a runner that ran out of disk, or a test that depends on timing. If you cannot tell a real failure from noise, people start re-running until green, and real problems pass through. What you will learn Reading a failing job from the first error, not the last line Reproducing a failure outside the pipeline Flaky tests, cache problems and drift between local and CI Debugging secrets and permissions without printing them Read the first error The last lines of a job often say only \"exit code 1\". The cause is higher up. Search the log for the first line containing error , failed or a stack trace, and check what ran just before it. Note the exact command, the working directory and the versions of tools printed at the start of the job. Reproduce it locally docker run -- rm -it -v \" $PWD \" :/work -w /work node:20.11 bash # same image as the job npm ci && npm test # same commands env | sort | grep -v -i -E &#x27;token|secret|key|pass&#x27; # compare environment, safely Use the same image tag, the same commands and a clean checkout. If it passes locally and fails in CI, compare tool versions, environment variables, file permissions, time zone, available memory and network access. Flaky or real? Sign Likely cause Fix Passes on retry, fails randomly Timing, order or shared state Remove sleeps, isolate data, fix the test Fails only on one runner Runner disk, memory or stale cache Clean the runner, pin its image Fails after a dependency release Unpinned versions Use a lockfile and pinned versions Fails near midnight or month end Date logic Inject the clock in tests Track flaky tests in a list with a count. A retry hides the symptom and keeps the cost, so use it only with a limit and a ticket. Caches and secrets A cache keyed only on the branch name can serve old build output. Key it on a hash of the lockfile. For secrets, check that the variable exists and is not empty without printing it: [ -n \" ${API_TOKEN:-} \" ] && echo \"API_TOKEN is set (length ${#API_TOKEN} )\" || echo \"API_TOKEN missing\" Masking hides values in logs but does not stop a script from encoding and printing them, so never enable shell tracing in jobs that handle secrets. Predict first. A test passes alone but fails when the whole suite runs. What is the most likely reason? Your guess Answer: Tests share state such as a database row, a file or a global variable, and one test changes what another expects. Run the failing test with the one before it, then isolate or reset the shared data. Random test ordering finds these problems early. Triage of a red pipeline Flow in order: 1, First error in the log to Reproduce locally; 2, Reproduce locally to Real or flaky; 3, Reproduce locally to Environment drift; 4, Real or flaky to Fix and prevent; 5, Environment drift to Fix and prevent. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. First error in the log Observability What it does: Find the earliest error, the exact command and tool versions. If it fails or is weak: The last line usually only repeats the exit code. Reproduce locally Step What it does: Run the same image and commands on a clean checkout. If it fails or is weak: A different local setup passes and proves nothing. Real or flaky Step What it does: Retry once and compare with recent runs on the same commit. If it fails or is weak: Endless retries hide a genuine defect. Environment drift Step What it does: Compare versions, variables, caches and runner state. If it fails or is weak: Unpinned tools change without any commit. Fix and prevent Step What it does: Add a pin, a test or a cache key so it cannot recur. If it fails or is weak: A one-time rerun leaves the cause in place. Incident: green yesterday, red today, same commit Incident The main branch pipeline fails at the build step with a compile error. No one changed the "}});